module lib/hf/eigen
  The symmetric eigensolver the SCF calls once per iteration: the Fock
  matrix, written in an orthonormal basis, is diagonalized, and its lowest
  eigenvectors are the occupied orbitals. Three solvers that share no
  numerical code (only the final sort and sign fix) sit in a registry, so
  the tests can hold each against the others: Householder reduction plus
  QL iteration (the one production runs), cyclic Jacobi, and the language's
  builtin `eig_sym`. Each returns `[n + 1, n]`: the eigenvalues in row `0`,
  ascending, and the eigenvectors as the columns of the rows after it.

eig_sort  @5587613a
  why   The SCF fills the lowest orbitals first, so it needs the eigenvalues
        in order; sorting the same way in every member lets their results be
        compared column by column.
  d  the eigenvalues, `[n]`, sorted in place
  V  the eigenvectors as columns, `[n, n]`, moved with their eigenvalues
  n  the matrix size
  i  the position being filled with the smallest remaining eigenvalue
  k  the column holding that smallest one
  p  its value
  j  a later position being compared
  t  the column held during the swap

eig_fix_signs  @22f4be6e
  why   An eigenvector is defined only up to its sign. Fixing the sign by one
        rule makes two members' vectors comparable, and keeps the SCF's
        orbitals from flipping between iterations.
  V    the eigenvectors as columns, flipped in place
  n    the matrix size
  k    the column being fixed
  big  the largest magnitude seen in that column so far
  at   the row where it was seen; the first one wins a tie
  i    the row being read

eig_pack  @d726f077
  why   The members of a registry must return one type; eigenvalues and
        vectors travel together in one array.
  d  the eigenvalues
  V  the eigenvectors as columns
  n  the matrix size
  R  the result, `[n + 1, n]`

eig_tred2  @f3c13a2c
  why   A symmetric matrix can be brought to tridiagonal form (nonzero only
        on the diagonal and next to it) by $n - 2$ reflections, and QL
        iteration on a tridiagonal matrix costs order $n$ per step instead of
        $n^2$. This is the standard route (EISPACK's tred2, in JAMA's form),
        kept close to its source so it can be checked line by line.
  V  on entry the symmetric matrix, transposed; on return the transpose of
     the orthogonal transformation that makes it tridiagonal, which QL goes
     on to rotate into the eigenvectors. Stored transposed so the inner
     loops walk along rows
  d  on return the diagonal of the tridiagonal matrix
  e  on return its subdiagonal, `e[i]` below `d[i]`, with `e[0]` unused
  n  the matrix size
  i  the row being reduced, from the last up
  scale  the sum of magnitudes of the row's entries left of the diagonal; the
         row is divided by it before squaring, so the squares cannot
         overflow or underflow
  h  the squared length of the scaled row; after the reflection, the
     reflection's normalizer
  f  an entry of the row, then running dot products
  g  $\pm$ the row's length, signed opposite to `f` so subtracting it does not
     cancel
  hh the correction that makes the reflected columns stay symmetric
  k  an index along the row
  j  a column being updated
  i2 the column whose transformation is being accumulated into `V`

  = d        start from the last row of the matrix
  = scale#2  add up the row's magnitudes left of the diagonal
  = e        a row that is already zero needs no reflection
  = g        the length of the scaled row, which becomes the new subdiagonal entry
  = hh       the symmetric update's correction: $K = f / 2h$ in the Householder formulas
  = i2       multiply the reflections together into `V`, from the smallest block up

eig_tql2  @d330e6e5
  why   QL with an implicit shift is the standard way to finish a
        tridiagonal eigenproblem: each eigenvalue converges quickly once the
        shift is close to it, and the rotations are applied to `V` as they
        happen, so the eigenvectors come out with the eigenvalues. JAMA's
        tql2, kept close to its source.
  V     on entry the transformation from eig_tred2 (transposed); on return the eigenvectors as rows
  d     on entry the diagonal; on return the eigenvalues, not sorted
  e     on entry the subdiagonal; used as work space
  n     the matrix size
  f     the sum of the shifts applied so far, added back to each eigenvalue
  tst1  the largest $|d_l| + |e_l|$ seen so far: a subdiagonal entry below
        machine epsilon times this counts as zero
  most  the largest number of QL steps any one eigenvalue needed
  l     the eigenvalue being found
  m     the first negligible subdiagonal entry at or after `l`: the block
        from `l` to `m` is the part still coupled to `l`
  it    the QL steps spent on this eigenvalue
  going `1` while the eigenvalue has not converged
  g     the current estimate of the eigenvalue
  p     the shift's intermediate, then the running value the rotations carry
  r     $\sqrt{p^2 + 1}$, then the length that each rotation normalizes
  dl1   the diagonal entry after `l` once the shift is applied
  h     the amount the shift moved the diagonal, then rotation work values
  c     the cosine of the current rotation; `c2` and `c3` keep the previous two
  s     the sine of the current rotation; `s2` keeps the previous one
  el1   the subdiagonal entry after `l`, kept for the final update
  i     the row of the rotation, moving up from `m - 1` to `l`
  k     the row of `V` a rotation is applied to

  = tst1#2  the scale that decides when a subdiagonal entry is negligible
  = m#2     look down the subdiagonal for the first negligible entry; the block above it is still coupled
  = p       the Wilkinson shift: the eigenvalue of the leading two by two block that is nearer $d_l$
  = f#2     subtract the shift from the rest of the block and remember it
  = s#2     one Givens rotation, chasing the bulge up from `m` to `l`
  = p#4     the new subdiagonal entry at `l`; the step has converged when it is negligible
  = going#2 stop when `e[l]` is negligible, or after `EIG_TQL2_MAXIT` steps
  = most#2  record the steps this eigenvalue took

eig_ql  @ca5f26ea
  why   The production member: Householder plus QL costs order $n^3$ with a
        small constant and is the method LAPACK and EISPACK use for dense
        symmetric matrices.
  A     the symmetric matrix, `[n, n]`
  B     not read; the stage signature is shared with the orthogonalizers
  opts  not read
  n     the matrix size
  V     `A` transposed, which the two phases turn into the eigenvectors as
        rows (so their inner loops walk along rows); transposed back at the end
  d     the eigenvalues
  e     the subdiagonal, work space

eig_jacobi_angle  @6f4f97c1
  why   One Jacobi rotation's angle, kept apart so the sweep reads as the
        loop over pairs.
  how   With `tau = (aqq - app) / (2 apq)` the tangent is the smaller root
        of `t^2 + 2 tau t - 1 = 0`, `t = sign(tau) / (|tau| + sqrt(1 +
        tau^2))` (Golub and Van Loan's Algorithm 8.5.1). For huge `|tau|` the root
        is `1 / (2 tau)`, which avoids squaring `tau`.
  app   the first diagonal entry of the block
  apq   the off-diagonal entry to zero, not zero
  aqq   the second diagonal entry
  tau   half the cotangent of twice the angle
  t     the tangent of the angle
  c     the cosine

eig_jacobi_raw  @01806116
  why   Jacobi works on the full matrix and never forms a tridiagonal, so a
        mistake in the Householder reduction cannot hide in both members.
  how   The cyclic Jacobi method of Golub and Van Loan (*Matrix
        Computations*, 4th ed., Algorithm 8.5.3), pairs in row order. Each
        rotation `W <- J^T W J` zeroes one pair and changes the two rows and
        columns it couples. The two diagonal entries move by `-t a_pq` and
        `+t a_pq` instead of through the row and column
        updates, which keeps their rounding to one operation. A pair is
        skipped and set to zero when `|a_pq| <= eps sqrt(|a_pp a_qq|)`, the
        relative test of Demmel and Veselic, or when it is below
        `eps^2 |A|_F`, which stands in for that test when a diagonal entry is
        zero. The module header gives the references.
  A     the symmetric matrix, not changed
  d     on return the eigenvalues, unsorted
  V     on return the eigenvectors as columns, the product of the rotations
  n     the matrix size
  floor_pq the absolute size below which a pair counts as zero
  sweeps the sweeps run so far
  rotated whether the current sweep has rotated a pair; a sweep without one ends the iteration
  p     the row of the pair
  q     its column
  w_pq  the pair's entry
  w_pp  the first diagonal entry it couples
  w_qq  the second
  c     the rotation's cosine
  s     its sine
  t     its tangent
  k     a row or column rotated along with the pair
  wkp   entry `(k, p)` before the rotation
  wkq   entry `(k, q)` before the rotation
  vkp   entry `(k, p)` of `V` before the rotation
  vkq   entry `(k, q)` of `V` before the rotation
  i     a diagonal position read out at the end

  = floor_pq  the absolute floor for a negligible pair
  = rotated#2 a new sweep starts with nothing rotated
  = w_pq      the pair to zero; an exact zero needs nothing
  = w_pp      read the block the rotation acts on, then find its angle
  = vkp       accumulate the rotation into `V`, whose columns become the eigenvectors

eig_jacobi  @d6eb7853
  why   The second opinion for eig_ql in the tests and the sweep.
  A     the symmetric matrix
  B     not read
