eig_methods  @9f0f0f61
  why   The SCF picks its eigensolver by index (`ScfOptions.eigensolver`),
        and a sweep can run every member on the same matrix.

eig_of  @6a169a82
  why   The one call the SCF makes to diagonalize, whichever member the
        options pick. It calls each member by name, so the default reaches
        the QL solver without a registry; `eig_methods()` lists the same
        members for the tests that run them side by side.
  A   the symmetric matrix
  k   the member's index in eig_methods
  m   `k` rounded: `EIG_QL`, `EIG_JACOBI` or `EIG_BUILTIN`

eig_values  @ecaaaa88
  R     a member's result

eig_vectors  @afe8f2e3
  R  a member's result

eig_residual  @503ece32
  why   Checks a result without any other solver: every eigenpair must
        satisfy $A v = \lambda v$.
  A      the symmetric matrix
  R      a member's result
  V      its eigenvectors as columns
  AV     $A V$
  worst  the largest entry of $A V - V \Lambda$ seen so far
  k      the eigenpair

eig_orth_defect  @24d5859c
  why   The eigenvectors of a symmetric matrix are orthonormal; the SCF's
        density assumes it.
  R      a member's result
  V      its eigenvectors as columns
  E      $V^\top V - I$
  a      a diagonal position

